WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.040
Every day, in every one of your cells, some cytosines lose their amino group and

2
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:17.060
turn into uracil. Uracil pairs with adenine, so the next replication puts an adenine

3
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.520
opposite it. The round after that leaves an A·T pair where a G·C pair had been.

4
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.340
That is a mutation made by chemistry alone.

5
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:33.200
So how does a cell find a few wrong bases among billions of right ones?

6
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:38.893
The answer lies in the double helix, which holds its information twice.

7
00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:45.073
If one strand is damaged, the cell cuts out the bad stretch and copies the partner

8
00:00:45.073 --> 00:00:51.913
strand. In base excision repair, a glycosylase flips the uracil out and cuts it from

9
00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:57.433
its sugar. Another enzyme nicks the backbone there, and a polymerase inserts the

10
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.933
correct nucleotide, using the partner strand.

11
00:01:00.773 --> 00:01:02.133
A ligase seals the nick.

12
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:05.773
Ultraviolet light does bulkier damage.

13
00:01:06.513 --> 00:01:11.673
It joins two neighboring pyrimidines on one strand into a dimer that bends the

14
00:01:11.673 --> 00:01:17.013
helix. Nucleotide excision repair senses the bend, not the chemistry.

15
00:01:17.773 --> 00:01:21.993
It cuts the damaged strand on both sides and removes a short piece.

16
00:01:22.753 --> 00:01:27.233
A polymerase fills the gap from the partner strand, and a ligase seals it.

17
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:35.913
A mismatch is two normal bases paired wrongly, such as G with T. Neither is damaged,

18
00:01:36.273 --> 00:01:40.433
so the cell must find the new strand, because the template was correct.

19
00:01:41.653 --> 00:01:48.353
In E. coli, the template carries methyl groups at GATC sites, and the new strand is

20
00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:49.893
briefly unmethylated.

21
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:57.433
MutS binds the mismatch, MutL joins it, and MutH nicks the unmethylated

22
00:01:57.433 --> 00:02:02.193
strand. That strand is removed past the mismatch and rebuilt.

23
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:08.557
A break through both strands removes the copying option at one spot.

24
00:02:09.557 --> 00:02:10.917
Picture a rope cut through.

25
00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:16.297
You can knot the ends, or splice in a matching length from an identical rope.

26
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:23.637
Non-homologous end joining knots the ends: a ring-shaped protein, Ku, grips each

27
00:02:23.637 --> 00:02:25.717
end and a ligase joins them.

28
00:02:26.397 --> 00:02:32.977
It uses no template, so if the ends needed trimming, a few bases may be gained or lost.

29
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:36.927
Homologous recombination splices from a matching rope.

30
00:02:37.947 --> 00:02:44.167
Nucleases trim the ends to single-stranded tails, and Rad51 coats them and invades

31
00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:45.387
a matching duplex.

32
00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:51.247
The invading end primes synthesis, copying the information lost at the break.

33
00:02:52.107 --> 00:02:56.987
The best template is the sister chromatid, the identical copy made in replication.

34
00:02:58.107 --> 00:03:03.387
One rule runs through almost all repair: the information is held twice.

